e because they're so complicated  to make which is what's so cool about  our medium but also  screws people over where this kind of  conversation is concerned  now i do think some things are probably  going to change out of everything that  happened here especially like  specifically with cd projekt red and i  think some of these things are good and  some of them are bad i think one of the  things that is a pro and a negative  is that they probably won't talk about  their game in progress so openly  again i mean the same thing phoned them  with the witcher 3 um  the way the game looked before it came  out and then afterwards people were very  very critical of just that things  changed over the development cycle and i  think being one of the  most open game studios that i've ever  encountered which as someone who  really likes talking to developers and  learning about their processes  especially again now that i work at a  dev studio i'm like  so interested in what other people are  making i think that that's something  that they will probably learn to cut off  this is a positive for consumers because  it means that they won't  say things that don't end up in the game  which can be  taken as marketing even if it's just  kind of devs chatting about what they're  making  you're still saying that publicly where  an audience can hear it there is a  negative fish consumers they're like 1  000  over um from my perspective having a dev  studio of that scale that was so  unrestrained  like second to none unrestrained  everyone else is so much more locked  down by their pr departments  um was great but i think we'll probably  lose that they will probably be a lot  more controlled by pr across the board  and ultimately that is going to help a  lot of people and of course i really  hope  again a thing that i would have done  differently is that on the press side  which for me i would include both  traditional journalists and youtubers  people will be a lot more transparent  about what preview builds actually are  there's usually a note  on on a lot of the big sites saying like  no this is subject to change the preview  build  but it is nowhere near explicit enough  that has not been communicated  anywhere near to the extent that it  should have been and truthfully i don't  think there's actually  any way um that press really can know  if a demo is going to turn out different  to the final game i i actually don't  think anyone  who played cyberpunk early and reported  on it could have known  that anything would change from what  they were told and i don't see that  changing i just don't know how it could  change  um maybe someone in the industry can  figure that out but i don't really see  an obvious solution there other than to  be much more explicit  when saying temporary expectations this  preview could be inaccurate things could  change very significantly  i think it especially occurs for games  that have been in development for a  really long time i guess audiences also  really have to listen for that to work  um that's a part of it  i really don't feel like anyone listened  uh when i said i was biased about the  game  um because people seem to have just  forgotten every time that i said that  now like i wish people had had heard i  was saying that for a really good reason  i knew i was really excited about this  game and that i am still a fan of it  that hasn't changed despite recognizing  some of this stuff is really  anti-consumer which brings me to  one sort of final point i've always been  very pro-review  and i hope that audiences are a little  bit more pro review  about this now too i wrote a thing for  ign way way way back about how i thought  it was really anti-consumer  that bethesda wasn't giving reviewers um  more than one day  with any of their games so they  basically couldn't accurately tell their  audiences if there were bugs if there  were features missing that were reported  on  um i i think it it's a bad bad bad  policy  for the public to not give some people  early access  to a game and there are a lot of really  hostile comments in response to that uh  ultimately a lot of  shut up i don't need reviewers i make my  own decisions you don't deserve early  access to anything cry harder ign  and there has also been a recent  consistent rhetoric uh  that is along the lines of even  accepting games early  makes you a chill but from my  perspective if people had had  early access to the playstation 4 and  xbox one  copies of the game which i would hope  that the public would try to put  pressure on the publisher for  then people would have known that there  were massive issues  with those particular versions of the  game they can warn people that the  product they're about to buy is really  broken i think ign  gave the console versions of four i  definitely tweeted that out  felt like a thing that that people  should know um and  restricting early access to those things  from my perspective  is a really awful practice i immediately  assume it's shady if a publisher isn't  giving people early access to a game i  immediately think that there is  something wrong  and i hope that general gamers  are more open to that line of thinking  going forward i do also want to know  that i did from the start  literally the first video where i spoke  about this wasn't quite as explicit  there but  i said it in ign videos as well say that  there was no way that this game was  going to run on xbox one and ps4  inexcusable i've also repeatedly said  that i think it's 100  cdpr's fault and that that is  inexcusable and super anti-consumer i am  not in support of it at all  but i definitely did explicitly say that  on more than one account there's no way  it was ever going to run in closing to  surmise a preview build is a plan for  what a game is supposed to be  it isn't always possible and that's  usually because of higher ups because of  management because of people trying to  make the money because 